# The Mabinogion page (mabinogion.html): what the tales are, the two great books, Lady Charlotte Guest,
# and all eleven tales. Tales that have their own legend page link to it (link="legend-...html").
# House rules (EDITORIAL.md): Arthur is a king, never "myth" or "legend".

INTRO_EN = [
 "## What is the Mabinogion?",
 "The Mabinogion is the name given to eleven tales written down in Welsh in the Middle Ages. They are among the finest stories of medieval Europe and some of the earliest prose stories written anywhere in Britain: tales of kings and enchanters, giants and shape-shifters, journeys to the Otherworld and hunts across the land, set in the real country of Wales, at Arberth and Harlech, in Ardudwy and Arfon, on the Preseli hills and the banks of the Severn.",
 "For centuries they were told aloud by the cyfarwyddiaid, the professional storytellers of the Welsh courts, who knew them by heart. They were written down between the eleventh and the thirteenth centuries, and the voice of the storyteller can still be heard in them: in the repeated phrases, the lists of names, and the endings that turn to the listener.",
 "Strictly, the Mabinogi is only the Four Branches, each of which ends with the words ‘and so ends this branch of the Mabinogi’. The form ‘Mabinogion’ appears just once in the manuscripts, but Lady Charlotte Guest chose it as the title of her translation, and it has been the name of the whole collection ever since. The word is probably built on mab, ‘boy’ or ‘son’, and may first have meant ‘tales of youth’, or simply ‘a tale’.",
 "## The White Book and the Red Book",
 "Almost everything we have of the tales comes from two great books, both more than six hundred years old.",
 "|| The White Book of Rhydderch | About 1350. Written for Rhydderch ab Ieuan Llwyd of Parc Rhydderch, near Llangeitho in Ceredigion, a great patron of Welsh poets. Now in the National Library of Wales, Aberystwyth.",
 "|| The Red Book of Hergest | About 1382 to 1410. Written mainly by the scribe Hywel Fychan for Hopcyn ap Tomas of Ynysforgan, near Swansea. Named after Hergest Court, near Kington, where it was kept for a time. Now in Jesus College, Oxford.",
 "|| Older pieces | Parts of Branwen and Manawydan, and of the romances, survive in Welsh hands of the thirteenth century, a hundred years before the two great books.",
 "Between them the two books hold the whole collection, and much more besides: Welsh poetry from Taliesin to the poets of the princes, the histories of the kings of Britain, the Triads, and books of law and medicine. The Dream of Rhonabwy survives in the Red Book alone. Without the care of the families who paid for these books, and the scribes who wrote them, the tales would be lost.",
 "## Lady Charlotte Guest",
 "The tales reached the wider world through a remarkable woman. Lady Charlotte Guest, 1812 to 1895, was the wife of John Josiah Guest, master of the great Dowlais ironworks at Merthyr Tudful. She came to Wales as a young bride, learned Welsh, and between 1838 and 1845 published the tales in Welsh and English, with long notes, in seven handsome parts.",
 "She worked with Welsh scholars: John Jones, Tegid, who had copied the Red Book by hand in Oxford, and Thomas Price, Carnhuanawc. She gave the collection its name, and to the eleven tales she added a twelfth, Hanes Taliesin, the tale of the poet’s birth, from a later manuscript. Her translation was read across Europe and America, and Tennyson took her Geraint for his Idylls of the King.",
 "The tales have been translated many times since, among them by Gwyn Jones and Thomas Jones in 1948 and by Sioned Davies in 2007, and they are read in the original Welsh in schools and universities across Wales.",
 "## Arthur in the tales",
 "Arthur holds court in five of the eleven tales. In Culhwch and Olwen, the oldest of them, his court is at Celli Wig in Cornwall and he is called ‘chief of the kings of this island’. In the Dream of Rhonabwy he is camped on the Severn on the eve of the battle of Badon. In the three romances his court is at Caerleon on Usk, in the heart of Gwent, the kingdom where Welsh tradition holds that Arthur ruled as Athrwys ap Meurig.",
]

INTRO_CY = [
 "## Beth yw’r Mabinogion?",
 "Y Mabinogion yw’r enw a roddir ar un chwedl ar ddeg a ysgrifennwyd yn Gymraeg yn yr Oesoedd Canol. Maent ymhlith straeon gorau Ewrop yr Oesoedd Canol a rhai o’r straeon rhyddiaith cynharaf a ysgrifennwyd yn unman ym Mhrydain: chwedlau am frenhinoedd a swynwyr, cewri a rhai sy’n newid eu ffurf, teithiau i Annwn a helfeydd ar draws y wlad, wedi’u gosod yng ngwlad go iawn Cymru, yn Arberth a Harlech, yn Ardudwy ac Arfon, ar y Preseli ac ar lannau Hafren.",
 "Am ganrifoedd fe’u hadroddwyd ar lafar gan y cyfarwyddiaid, storïwyr proffesiynol llysoedd Cymru, a’u gwyddai ar eu cof. Fe’u hysgrifennwyd rhwng yr unfed ganrif ar ddeg a’r drydedd ganrif ar ddeg, a gellir clywed llais y cyfarwydd ynddynt o hyd: yn yr ymadroddion a ailadroddir, y rhestrau o enwau, a’r diweddglo sy’n troi at y gwrandawr.",
 "A siarad yn fanwl, y Mabinogi yw’r Pedair Cainc yn unig, a phob un yn gorffen â’r geiriau ‘ac felly y terfyna’r gainc hon o’r Mabinogi’. Unwaith yn unig y ceir y ffurf ‘Mabinogion’ yn y llawysgrifau, ond fe’i dewiswyd gan yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest yn deitl i’w chyfieithiad, a dyna enw’r casgliad cyfan byth ers hynny. Mae’n debyg mai ar y gair mab y seiliwyd y gair, ac efallai mai ‘chwedlau ieuenctid’, neu ‘chwedl’ yn syml, oedd ei ystyr gyntaf.",
 "## Y Llyfr Gwyn a’r Llyfr Coch",
 "Daw bron y cwbl sydd gennym o’r chwedlau o ddau lyfr mawr, y ddau dros chwe chan mlwydd oed.",
 "|| Llyfr Gwyn Rhydderch | Tua 1350. Ysgrifennwyd i Rydderch ab Ieuan Llwyd o Barc Rhydderch, ger Llangeitho yng Ngheredigion, noddwr mawr i feirdd Cymru. Heddiw yn Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru, Aberystwyth.",
 "|| Llyfr Coch Hergest | Tua 1382 hyd 1410. Ysgrifennwyd yn bennaf gan y copïydd Hywel Fychan i Hopcyn ap Tomas o Ynysforgan, ger Abertawe. Fe’i henwyd ar ôl Llys Hergest, ger Ceintun, lle’i cadwyd am gyfnod. Heddiw yng Ngholeg Iesu, Rhydychen.",
 "|| Darnau hŷn | Mae rhannau o Branwen a Manawydan, ac o’r rhamantau, wedi goroesi mewn llawysgrifau Cymraeg o’r drydedd ganrif ar ddeg, gan mlynedd cyn y ddau lyfr mawr.",
 "Rhyngddynt mae’r ddau lyfr yn cadw’r casgliad cyfan, a llawer mwy: barddoniaeth Gymraeg o Daliesin hyd at Feirdd y Tywysogion, hanes brenhinoedd Prydain, y Trioedd, a llyfrau cyfraith a meddygaeth. Yn y Llyfr Coch yn unig y goroesodd Breuddwyd Rhonabwy. Heb ofal y teuluoedd a dalodd am y llyfrau hyn, a’r copïwyr a’u hysgrifennodd, byddai’r chwedlau wedi’u colli.",
 "## Yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest",
 "Cyrhaeddodd y chwedlau’r byd ehangach drwy wraig hynod. Roedd yr Arglwyddes Charlotte Guest, 1812 hyd 1895, yn wraig i John Josiah Guest, meistr gwaith haearn mawr Dowlais ym Merthyr Tudful. Daeth i Gymru’n briodferch ifanc, dysgodd Gymraeg, a rhwng 1838 ac 1845 cyhoeddodd y chwedlau yn Gymraeg ac yn Saesneg, gyda nodiadau hir, mewn saith rhan gain.",
 "Gweithiodd gydag ysgolheigion Cymreig: John Jones, Tegid, a oedd wedi copïo’r Llyfr Coch â llaw yn Rhydychen, a Thomas Price, Carnhuanawc. Rhoddodd hi ei enw i’r casgliad, ac at yr un chwedl ar ddeg ychwanegodd ddeuddegfed, Hanes Taliesin, stori geni’r bardd, o lawysgrif ddiweddarach. Darllenwyd ei chyfieithiad ar draws Ewrop ac America, a chymerodd Tennyson ei Geraint hi ar gyfer ei Idylls of the King.",
 "Cyfieithwyd y chwedlau lawer gwaith ers hynny, gan gynnwys gan Gwyn Jones a Thomas Jones yn 1948 a chan Sioned Davies yn 2007, ac fe’u darllenir yn y Gymraeg wreiddiol mewn ysgolion a phrifysgolion ledled Cymru.",
 "## Arthur yn y chwedlau",
 "Mae Arthur yn cynnal llys mewn pump o’r un chwedl ar ddeg. Yn Culhwch ac Olwen, yr hynaf ohonynt, mae ei lys yng Nghelli Wig yng Nghernyw a’i enw yw ‘penteyrnedd yr ynys hon’. Ym Mreuddwyd Rhonabwy mae’n gwersylla ar lan Hafren ar drothwy brwydr Baddon. Yn y tair rhamant mae ei lys yng Nghaerllion ar Wysg, yng nghalon Gwent, y deyrnas lle, yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, y teyrnasai Arthur fel Athrwys ap Meurig.",
]

